    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d45">

                <pb facs="0153" n="41" sameAs="frus:frus1918/pg41"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 835.73/63</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stimson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-03-29T19:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 29, 1918, 7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>John L. Merrill, vice president of the Central &amp; South American Telegraph
                    Co., has informed the Department that by a decree dated February 21 and
                    published in the <hi rendition="#italic">Official Bulletin</hi> of March 5 the
                    Argentine Government authorized transitorily the River Plate Telegraph Co. to
                    collect three francs per word on telegrams received from North America destined
                    for Brazil, excepting the Amazon Telegraph Co. and wireless stations.</p>
                <p>Ask the Argentine Government why this apparent discrimination is made against
                    telegrams coming from North America, and cable Department explanation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
